<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kornelimünster</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kornelimünster is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> that grew around a Benedictine abbey founded in 814 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-aniane">Benedict of Aniane</a>, at a place then called Inda. The abbey took its new name from relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-cornelius">Pope Cornelius</a> received in 875, and its abbots ruled a small imperial territory until 1802. Its abbey church, which keeps relics associated with the Passion, remains a pilgrimage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kornelimunster.jpg" alt="Kornelimünster" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kornelimuenster_Markt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
